# .gitignore for the claude-klabauter published repo root.
#
# Shipped FROM `dist/klabauter-toplevel/` via the `claude-klabauter-publish-repo-toplevel`
# flat-mirror row (publish-targets.portable) -- see the sibling `.percolate-ignore` in this
# directory for why it moved here instead of staying an (never-authored) destination-native
# file. Fixes the unscanned-published-check noise measured live (499 findings, all
# `__pycache__/*.pyc`, on one real `claude-klabauter-publish-repo-toplevel` run): anything
# that runs Python inside a fresh clone of the published repo (pytest, a stray `python -m`)
# accumulates untracked bytecode-cache noise otherwise.

# --- Python build artifacts ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/

# --- Coordinator working state, never OSS payload ---
# No publish row ships `state/` here. When it appears it is a stray session
# writing its own orientation cache or session-hierarchy JSON into the mirror
# (observed 2026-08-10 and again 2026-08-11) -- internal content that must not
# reach a public repo via a blanket `git add`.
state/

# `state/` is not a stray in the sense the paragraph above assumed, and the
# distinction matters to whoever next tries to stop it reappearing: the
# writers are not doing a blanket `git add`, they are rooting themselves on
# THE CWD'S GIT ROOT. `engine_provenance_counter.record_engine_provenance`
# resolves `resolve_git_root_cheap(cwd)` and appends under `<that
# root>/state/`; `sweep-boot`'s orientation cache, the group-EM watch, and
# the session-hierarchy and test-red writers take the same shape. So ANY
# session that runs with cwd inside the mirror -- routine maintenance, a
# stray test run -- leaves state behind, and no ignore rule prevents that.
# Ignoring is the whole remedy available here; the tree is swept when it
# grows (2026-09-02: 19 files removed, archived to claude-klabauter
# `state/audits/2026-09-02-claude-klabauter-mirror-state-residue/`).
# Eliminating it means changing where those writers root, across several
# writers -- a design change, not an ignore entry.

# Coordinator scratch, same class as `state/`: cwd-rooted working space, no
# publish row ships it, never payload. Present in the mirror on 2026-09-02
# (`scratch/benchmark-clones/`) and unignored until then -- it stayed out of
# `git status` only because it happened to be EMPTY, and git does not report
# empty directories. One file inside it would have made it a dirty-dest
# finding on every round.
scratch/

# Same class, two more strays observed 2026-08-15: a session's own `.claude/`
# scaffolding, and `Saved/ProjectRag/` -- a ~190MB example-retrieval-repo index (graph.db
# plus structural sqlite and its -shm/-wal siblings) left by someone indexing
# the mirror. Neither is OSS payload. Untracked-but-unignored is the worst of
# both here: it never publishes, but it does trip percolate-round's dirty-dest
# check, whose offered remedy (`--reconcile-dest=discard`) resets the mirror to
# HEAD and removes residue -- which would delete an expensive index this repo
# does not own and cannot regenerate.
.claude/
Saved/

# A provisioned fleet environment, basename `.fleet-env` (docs/reference/
# fleet-shared-environment-contract.md § DECISIONS). Same class as `Saved/`
# above but a multi-GB Python environment rather than a ~190MB index -- must
# never publish, and per `--reconcile-dest=discard`'s remedy
# (`percolate-round.py::_reconcile_dest_discard`, `git reset --hard HEAD`
# then `git clean -fd`, no `-x`) an ignored path survives that reconcile, so
# ignoring it here is what keeps a provisioned environment from being
# destroyed by the standard dirty-dest recovery path. `.fleet-env.prior/` is
# the rollback copy left by a fleet-env migration in flight -- same class,
# same remedy, and NOT covered by the `.fleet-env/` entry above (sibling
# directory, not a sub-path) -- so it needs the same explicit ignore.
.fleet-env/
.fleet-env.prior/

# Two more members of the same family, both left in the mirror by a real run and
# neither covered by the two entries above (sibling names, not sub-paths).
# `.fleet-env.gen-<pid>-<hex>/` is the generation directory a fleet-env build
# populates before cutover (`fleet_env._gen_root`); an abandoned build leaves it
# behind until `_sweep_orphan_generations` reclaims it, and it is the same
# multi-GB never-publish class as `.fleet-env/` itself. `.fleet-env.lock` is the
# advisory build-lock sidecar (`fleet_env`, `Path(str(env_root) + ".lock")`) --
# deliberately never unlinked, so it is present in every provisioned checkout
# forever. Untracked-but-unignored, it trips percolate-round's dirty-dest check
# on every single round, and `--reconcile-dest=discard`'s `git clean -fd` would
# then delete a lock another session may be holding right then. Anchored with a
# leading `/` so it cannot reach the shipped payload lockfile of the same stem,
# `docs/install/fleet-env.lock`.
.fleet-env.gen-*/
/.fleet-env.lock

# publish.py's own staging directories -- `.<dest-basename>.publish-staging-<hex>/`
# (`_create_publish_staging_dir`, a tempfile.mkdtemp beside the destination) and the
# `.prior` rollback copy a swap renames aside. Never payload: publish populates one,
# swaps it into place, and sweeps it. Absent from this file until 2026-08-26, 4045 of
# their files reached the mirror's HEAD in one accidental blanket `git add` -- which
# then broke EVERY subsequent round, because the commit leg's un-stage of that many
# paths exceeds Windows' 32767-char command line (WinError 206).
#
# Ignoring is also what makes `--reconcile-dest=discard` safe here: its `git clean -fd`
# carries no `-x`, so an ignored staging directory survives a dirty-dest recovery
# rather than being deleted out from under a round that is still writing it -- the same
# reasoning the `.fleet-env` entries above turn on.
#
# Trailing slash is load-bearing: DIRECTORY segments only, never a basename. A shipped
# payload FILE named `x-publish-staging-y.py` is real content, the same distinction
# `percolate/surface.py::PUBLISH_STAGING_DIR_RE` and its callers already draw.
*publish-staging-*/

# The round's own state directory -- `.percolate/round-manifest.json`
# (`round.default_manifest_path`), one file per dest repo, overwritten every
# round. Working memory, not published product: 62KB of payload paths that
# describe how the last round was computed. Tracking it makes every round's
# manifest a diff in the next round, and leaves it declared by no row, so the
# removal side reads it as deletable and would churn it delete-then-rewrite
# forever.
#
# RULING, claude-central-em 2026-08-26, for BOTH mirrors: a path that should
# never be tracked is ignored, not repeatedly deleted -- do not lean on the
# removal rule to clear it. Untracked-but-unignored is the state this closes:
# it was one `git add -A` away from being committed on coordinator-claude, and
# was already tracked at HEAD on claude-klabauter.
/.percolate/

# --- Editor / OS detritus ---
*.bak
*.tmp
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.orig
*.rej
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
